871926308,-0.8624873161315918,-1.680836794548668e-05,-0.00028081765049137175,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-3.576278118089249e-07,0.0,-0.0010251511121168733,-0.04845605045557022,0.0,-9.536738616588991e-07,-0.0032480601221323013,0.0,-0.0024825488217175007,0.0,0.0,-2.884823152271565e-05,0.0,-3.3378546504536644e-06,0.0,0.0,-6.12716976320371e-05,-0.002889032242819667,-1.1920922133867862e-06,-4.3748852476710454e-05,-2.264974000354414e-06,0.0,-5.245195097813848e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-1.6689286894688848e-06,-5.960462772236497e-07,-3.933898824470816e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"To determine the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the normal vector to the plane and a point on the plane.\n\nFirst, identify a point on the line. When \\(t = 0\\), the point is \\(\\mathbf{v}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). So, the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane.\n\nNext, find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the coefficients of \\(t\\) in the parameterized equation, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of the direction vector of the line and any vector that lies in the plane. We can use the vector from the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to another point on the line, say \\(\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\n","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,1555,5733,1506,553,198,59,26056,10374,13233,90,85,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,488,259,24984,220,18,83,24984,220,16,481,259,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,60,48101,304,264,11031,315,279,1352,400,87,488,379,488,17988,284,294,2418,220,11252,279,11457,6716,4930,66,11991,568,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198,1249,8253,279,11457,6716,1124,1188,66,11,294,10699,8,369,279,11031,23606,17767,87,488,379,488,17988,284,294,57758,429,5610,279,1555,5733,1506,553,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,85,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,488,259,24984,220,18,83,24984,220,16,481,259,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,701,582,1184,311,1477,279,4622,4621,311,279,11031,323,264,1459,389,279,11031,382,5338,11,10542,264,1459,389,279,1555,13,3197,17767,83,284,220,15,59,701,279,1459,374,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,85,25547,15,8,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,15,24984,220,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,568,2055,11,279,1459,1124,1188,16,11,220,15,11,220,16,10699,8,15448,389,279,11031,382,5847,11,1477,279,5106,4621,315,279,1555,13,576,5106,4621,374,2661,553,279,36829,315,17767,83,57758,304,279,5733,1506,23606,11,892,374,1124,11520,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,18,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,3593,785,4622,4621,311,279,11031,646,387,1730,553,4633,279,5312,1985,315,279,5106,4621,315,279,1555,323,894,4621,429,15448,304,279,11031,13,1205,646,990,279,4621,504,279,1459,1124,1188,16,11,220,15,11,220,16,10699,8,311,2441,1459,389,279,1555,11,1977,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,85,25547,16,8,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,220,18,24984,220,15,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,568,576,4621,374,1124,11520,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,220,18,24984,220,15,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,481,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,15,24984,220,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,18,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,3593],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe line parameterized by\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\]lies in a plane of the form $x + y + cz = d.$  Enter the ordered pair $(c,d).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"1399635d2d6572e5d76a1cf4052c5a280e426c60e1f44ed93d39a3ce452587a8"},"seconds":35.480140209198,"seed":15994890039637181,"stop":"eos","text":"Since the direction vector of the line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need another vector in the plane to take the cross product. We can use the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and another direction vector, for example, the vector from \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to \\(\\mathbf{v}(-1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane is the cross product of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(3 \\cdot 1 - (-1) \\cdot (-3)) - \\mathbf{j}(1 \\cdot 1 - (-1) \\cdot (-1)) + \\mathbf{k}(1 \\cdot (-3) - 3 \\cdot (-1)) = \\mathbf{i}(3 - 3) - \\mathbf{j}(1 - 1) + \\mathbf{k}(-3 + 3) = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis is incorrect, so we use the direction vector of the line and another direction vector in the plane, for example, \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), which gives us the normal vector as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThus, the plane equation is \\(x + y + z = d\\). Substituting the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) into the plane equation, we get \\(1 + 0 + 1 = d\\), so \\(d = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(1, 2)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(1, 2)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To determine the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the normal vector to the plane and a point on the plane.\n\nFirst, identify a point on the line. When \\(t = 0\\), the point is \\(\\mathbf{v}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). So, the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane.\n\nNext, find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the coefficients of \\(t\\) in the parameterized equation, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of the direction vector of the line and any vector that lies in the plane. We can use the vector from the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to another point on the line, say \\(\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSince the direction vector of the line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need another vector in the plane to take the cross product. We can use the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and another direction vector, for example, the vector from \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to \\(\\mathbf{v}(-1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane is the cross product of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(3 \\cdot 1 - (-1) \\cdot (-3)) - \\mathbf{j}(1 \\cdot 1 - (-1) \\cdot (-1)) + \\mathbf{k}(1 \\cdot (-3) - 3 \\cdot (-1)) = \\mathbf{i}(3 - 3) - \\mathbf{j}(1 - 1) + \\mathbf{k}(-3 + 3) = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis is incorrect, so we use the direction vector of the line and another direction vector in the plane, for example, \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), which gives us the normal vector as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThus, the plane equation is \\(x + y + z = d\\). Substituting the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) into the plane equation, we get \\(1 + 0 + 1 = d\\), so \\(d = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(1, 2)}\\).","id":"precalculus:264"}
